{"author":{"id":"f21006d1826991d76b358f177209294474e8c2701fc659ff8fe8b8f40ee77f55"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1773599096,"nonce":"ihc2Kt7iwOh2NOUVJB1meGUu+0o=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOCGKeh87yNZx2","github-url":"https://github.com/git-bug/git-bug/issues/1536#issuecomment-4063599734"},"message":"\u003e Works, thx\n\ni'm glad it's working for you!\n\nas mentioned, though, all this is doing is fixing the ordering so that we don't attempt to open the browser before the http server is running -- your gif recording still perplexes me.\n\nyou ran the command, and your browser opened to a 'failed to connect to server' page. this was somewhat expected (or at least, common), because the browser could try rendering the page (and fail) before the http server is actually started. **however**, all i've ever done to work around this is simply refresh the page after a second or two. when you tried refreshing the page -- even after a full minute -- you still got the error.\n\nthis means that either:\n\n- the web server had failed to start, **or**\n- your browser / system was unable to reach the web server, **or**\n- your browser had cached the initial request (the failed request) and decided to just keep showing that to you instead of actually trying to hit the server again\n\nthe last case is very highly unlikely, but a possibility (however small). i'd be very curious to find out what the root cause of your issue was -- but i'm glad that the changes to the opening sequence works for you :)\n\ni'm going to close this issue for now, but please let us know if you do encounter an issue running the web server in the future.","files":null},{"type":4,"timestamp":1773599137,"nonce":"6x+2nkk6ffXzHAeb8MMDPxzGIWY=","metadata":{"github-id":"CE_lADOCGKeh87y3y7TzwAAAAV95pzd"},"status":2}]}